INT. RURAL PHARMACY - COUNTER - DAY

A tiny drugstore: wooden floorboards, a spinning card rack, an electric fan. Behind the counter, shelves of amber bottles and a handwritten "No Credit" sign.

WINIFRED OYELARAN-SCOTT (50s, white coat, reading glasses on her head) counts pills onto a tray. ELMER HOLLOWAY (70s, overalls, seed cap) leans on the counter with both elbows. GABRIEL FONTANA-ABARA (30s, delivery driver, clipboard) waits behind him, shifting his weight.

WINIFRED
Elmer, you're on the wrong dosage.

ELMER
Gabriel says it's right.

GABRIEL
I said it was what the doctor wrote.

WINIFRED
Doctors write all sorts of things.

ELMER
Winifred, I've taken these for twenty years.

WINIFRED
And the dosage changed in March.

GABRIEL
It changed in February.

WINIFRED
It changed in March, Gabriel. I have the paper.

ELMER
Then show me the paper.

WINIFRED
It's in the back.

GABRIEL
I'll fetch it.

WINIFRED
You'll do no such thing.

GABRIEL
It's my job to deliver.

WINIFRED
Your job is to hand me the bottle.

Gabriel sets a bottle on the counter. Winifred picks it up, squints, reads the label, and sets it down again.

WINIFRED (CONT'D)
This is the wrong strength.

ELMER
See?

GABRIEL
It came from the warehouse.

WINIFRED
It came from your truck.

ELMER
She's got you, son.

GABRIEL
Elmer, whose side are you on?

ELMER
My own.

Winifred sighs, pulls a fresh bottle off the shelf, and labels it in a small, steady hand.

WINIFRED
One tablet. Morning. With food.

ELMER
I eat food.

WINIFRED
Eat more of it.

She slides the bottle across. Elmer takes it, tips his cap, and shuffles to the door.

GABRIEL
Winifred, I'm sorry.

WINIFRED
Don't be. Just check twice.

GABRIEL
Yes, ma'am.

The fan hums. The cards in the rack tick, one against another. Nothing else moves.
